and for the Infinitive Liu. xxi. 8. 8 Poeno cepisse tam se urbem, st paulum adnitatur, credente. For abisse = ‘ pass away’ cf. Fam. ix. 20. 1 ///a mea quae solebas antea laudare abierunt. The only other interpretation which seems possible is Santen’s ‘for the fates knew that he had not gone away for long, if he went to Troy ;’ so soon would he be sent back dead. He compares Prop. ili. 12. 13 ewe aliquid de te flendum referatur in urna. Sic redeunt wllts qui cectdere loc’s. For the abl. of duration of time see Drager 1. p. 493. This would make the passage very like a Tragic fragm. quoted by Santen from Tusc. Disp. iii. 13. 28 Praeterea ad Trovam cum mist ob defendendam Graeciam, Scibam me in mortiferum bellum, non in epulas mittere : but the suppression of the explanatory clause is intolerably harsh, as taken by themselves the words, if the subject is Protesilaus, suggest the exactly opposite meaning, viz. that he would soon come back alive, ine, as a conqueror. Some inferior MSS give adesse, a word often confused with adzsse, e.g. in the Copa 5 where see Ribbeck: the meaning would then be ‘which severing of the marriage-tie was not very distant.’ So A. Weise and B. Schmidt. Munro also adopts adesse, making Quod refer to abreplum coniugtum, ‘ which loss of her husband the Fates well knew was not far away.’ Bahrens conj. odzsse, L. Miiller sczrant, ‘had deter-mined,’ which accords with the words (perhaps from Euripides’ Protesi-laus') of Eustathius on 1]. ii. 7o1 χρησμοῦ δοθέντος πρῶτον ἐν Τροίᾳ πεσεῖν τὸν προπηδήσαντα τῆς νηός (see Kiessling Analecta Catulliana, p. 6), but would require a future or at least a present infinitive. | scibant. Lucr. v. 934, cf. audibant LXXXIV. 8, custodibant LXIV. 319. Ov. Heroid. Xili. 93 Sors quogue nescto quem fato destgnat iniquo Qui primus Danaum Troada tangat humum. Auson. Epitaph. Heroum 13 Schenkl Fa/ale adscriptum nomen mtht Protesilao... Quid queror? hoc letum tam tum mea Sata canebant, Tale mthi nomen cum pater imposurt. (A. 